48
00:02:26,938 --> 00:02:30,685
Right from the start, Rashi frames tithing not

49
00:02:30,738 --> 00:02:33,085
as a mere tax, but as part of

50
00:02:33,165 --> 00:02:37,619
a covenantal relationship between God and the people.Next,

51
00:02:37,659 --> 00:02:40,179
let's look at one of Rashi's most psychologically

52
00:02:40,259 --> 00:02:42,899
rich comments, on the laws of giving to

53
00:02:42,919 --> 00:02:43,739
the poor.

54
00:02:44,019 --> 00:02:46,980
The Torah says, "You shall not harden your

55
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,700
heart, nor shut your hand from your needy

56
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:53,620
brother." Rashi breaks this into two distinct human

57
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:54,820
tendencies.

58
00:02:54,940 --> 00:02:58,420
There are people, he says, who agonize internally

59
00:02:58,487 --> 00:03:01,060
