60
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:02,980
in painful deliberation.

61
00:03:03,108 --> 00:03:05,780
That is the hardened heart.

62
00:03:05,908 --> 00:03:08,500
Then there are people who actually reach out

63
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:11,540
their hand, showing readiness to give, but then

64
00:03:11,588 --> 00:03:13,620
pull it back and close it.

65
00:03:13,796 --> 00:03:15,700
That is the shut hand.

66
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:19,781
The Torah addresses both types because generosity can

67
00:03:19,861 --> 00:03:22,821
fail at either stage, in the mind or

68
00:03:22,874 --> 00:03:23,941
in the action.

69
00:03:24,381 --> 00:03:26,830
Rashi is reading the Torah as a keen

70
00:03:26,883 --> 00:03:30,510
observer of human nature, recognizing that resistance to

71
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:33,150
giving is not one simple thing but a

72
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:37,536
complex inner drama.Staying with the theme of charity,

73
00:03:37,576 --> 00:03:39,541
the Torah instructs us to provide for the

74
00:03:39,621 --> 00:03:42,741
poor person "sufficient for his need, in that

75
00:03:42,794 --> 00:03:46,341
which he lacks." Rashi takes this phrase and

76
00:03:46,411 --> 00:03:48,021
expands it dramatically.

77
00:03:48,234 --> 00:03:50,501
It means, he says, that if the person

78
00:03:50,561 --> 00:03:53,221
was once accustomed to riding a horse with

79
00:03:53,381 --> 00:03:56,501
a servant running before him, you are obligated

80
00:03:56,554 --> 00:03:58,101
to provide even that.

81
00:03:58,301 --> 00:04:02,501
The word "lo," meaning "for him," Rashi connects

82
00:04:02,554 --> 00:04:05,141
to the verse in Genesis where God says,

83
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:08,021
"I will make a helper for him," teaching

84
00:04:08,053 --> 00:04:10,341
that you must even help the poor person

85
00:04:10,421 --> 00:04:11,621
find a spouse.

86
00:04:11,781 --> 00:04:15,061
This is a remarkable standard of generosity.

87
00:04:15,328 --> 00:04:17,781
Rashi is telling us that caring for the

88
00:04:17,861 --> 00:04:19,941
poor is not just about survival.

89
00:04:20,074 --> 00:04:23,061
It is about restoring a person's dignity, their

90
00:04:23,114 --> 00:04:26,714
sense of self, their place in the world.Now

91
00:04:26,734 --> 00:04:29,034
let's turn to what seems like a contradiction

92
00:04:29,087 --> 00:04:29,834
in the text.

93
00:04:30,047 --> 00:04:32,876
In verse four, the Torah says, "There shall

94
00:04:32,929 --> 00:04:35,356
be no needy among you." But just a

95
00:04:35,396 --> 00:04:38,236
few verses later, in verse eleven, it says,

96
00:04:38,436 --> 00:04:40,716
"For the needy shall never cease out of

97
00:04:40,736 --> 00:04:43,756
the land." Rashi resolves this beautifully.

98
00:04:43,852 --> 00:04:46,556
When you do God's will, he explains, the

99
00:04:46,636 --> 00:04:49,276
needy will exist among others but not among

100
00:04:49,336 --> 00:04:49,676
you.

101
00:04:49,776 --> 00:04:52,036
But when you fail to do God's will,

102
00:04:52,086 --> 00:04:53,596
the needy will be among you.

103
00:04:53,783 --> 00:04:56,476
So both verses are true, but they describe

104
00:04:56,516 --> 00:04:59,356
different realities depending on the moral choices of

105
00:04:59,376 --> 00:05:00,236
the community.

106
00:05:00,376 --> 00:05:04,156
The apparent contradiction becomes a conditional promise, and

107
00:05:04,236 --> 00:05:07,436
Rashi transforms a textual puzzle into a profound

108
00:05:07,496 --> 00:05:11,013
ethical teaching.Moving to the laws of the Hebrew

109
00:05:11,083 --> 00:05:13,735
servant, the Torah commands that when you free

110
00:05:13,815 --> 00:05:17,575
a servant, "you shall furnish him liberally." The

111
00:05:17,632 --> 00:05:21,335
Hebrew word used here is ha'anek, and Rashi

112
00:05:21,406 --> 00:05:24,455
explains it refers to something conspicuous, like a

113
00:05:24,519 --> 00:05:27,335
neck ornament, something placed high on the body

114
00:05:27,375 --> 00:05:28,935
where it is easily seen.

115
00:05:29,155 --> 00:05:31,895
The idea is that your generosity should be

116
00:05:31,965 --> 00:05:34,775
visible and public, making it clear that you

117
00:05:34,807 --> 00:05:36,535
have treated this person well.

118
00:05:36,775 --> 00:05:40,038
And Rashi adds another layer: the Torah says

119
00:05:40,065 --> 00:05:42,625
to remember that you were once a slave

120
00:05:42,678 --> 00:05:45,265
in Egypt, and God loaded you with good

121
00:05:45,334 --> 00:05:48,065
things, from the spoils of Egypt and from

122
00:05:48,105 --> 00:05:49,825
the spoils at the Red Sea.

123
00:05:50,081 --> 00:05:53,265
Just as God gave to you abundantly and

124
00:05:53,323 --> 00:05:56,225
repeatedly, so you must give to the departing

125
00:05:56,295 --> 00:05:59,745
servant, loading him up not once but again

126
00:05:59,825 --> 00:06:03,974
and again.Finally, let's look at Rashi's comment on

127
00:06:03,994 --> 00:06:05,094
the festivals.

128
00:06:05,238 --> 00:06:08,694
When discussing Sukkot, the Feast of Tabernacles, the

129
00:06:08,734 --> 00:06:12,855
Torah says "you shall only be rejoicing." Rashi

130
00:06:12,908 --> 00:06:15,095
notes that on its plain level, this is

131
00:06:15,135 --> 00:06:17,815
not actually a command but a promise, an

132
00:06:17,879 --> 00:06:19,735
assurance that you will be joyful.

133
00:06:19,915 --> 00:06:22,695
But the Rabbis derived from this phrasing a

134
00:06:22,775 --> 00:06:26,055
legal obligation, that the night before the final

135
00:06:26,115 --> 00:06:28,615
