137
00:06:30,999 --> 00:06:33,975
This is a beautiful example of how Rashi

138
00:06:34,028 --> 00:06:37,735
holds two readings simultaneously: the simple meaning of

139
00:06:37,755 --> 00:06:40,535
the text as a divine assurance of happiness,

140
00:06:40,635 --> 00:06:43,895
and the rabbinic reading that transforms joy itself

141
00:06:43,959 --> 00:06:49,347
into a sacred obligation.Stepping back, what emerges from

142
00:06:49,427 --> 00:06:52,067
Rashi's commentary on this portion is a vision

143
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:55,827
of a society built on radical responsibility toward

144
00:06:55,896 --> 00:06:56,467
others.

145
00:06:56,567 --> 00:07:00,867
Whether it is tithing, lending, freeing servants, or

146
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:05,987
celebrating festivals, Rashi consistently emphasizes that every law

147
00:07:06,047 --> 00:07:07,507
